<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<packages>
  <!-- v1.4.0.32: the metapackage is REMOVED from packages.config. It ships no compile-time
       assemblies for UAP; its netcore50 facade DLLs (System.Runtime, Version=4.0.0.0) sitting
       in ..\packages are picked up by MSBuild's simple-name resolution and SHADOW the correct
       SDK reference-assembly facades -> XamlCompiler WMC1006 "Cannot resolve System.Runtime.dll"
       + WMC9999 "Type universe cannot resolve assembly: System.Runtime". The classic UAP project
       system resolves ALL BCL/WinRT references itself from the Windows SDK (UnionMetadata +
       ReferenceAssemblies); NuGet is needed only for third-party libs. Runtime .NET Core IL
       still comes from the machine-wide VC++ 2015-2019 UWP Desktop runtime (VS2017 installs it).
       If restore ever needs to be re-enabled, do NOT return this line - see BUILD_VS2017.md. -->
  <!-- v1.4.0.34 FINAL FIX for WMC1006/WMC9999 'System.Runtime'. Postmortem of v1.4.0.33
       (clean tree, metapackage already gone, error persisted): the two remaining NuGet
       libs were referenced from their netstandard1.3 folders. Verified byte-level facts:
       - Newtonsoft.Json netstandard1.3 asset carries TargetFramework(".NETStandard,v1.3")
         => its compile-time closure = .NET Standard 1.3 package graph => hundreds of
         nuget-packaged System.* reference facades that are NOT part of a UAP 15063 SDK
         install; XamlCompiler's type universe demands them, fails on the very first
         (System.Runtime, Version=4.0.0.0) -> exactly WMC1006 + WMC9999.
       - DocumentFormat.OpenXml 2.9.1 ships ONLY netstandard1.3 (+desktop) assets - no
         netstandard2.0 exists for that version at all (verified against nuget.org).
       Fix: drop both packages entirely. Newtonsoft.Json is replaced by a vendored
       zero-dependency mini serializer (Helpers\JsonSerializer.cs); the xlsx import/export
       (ExcelHelper) is rewritten on top of System.IO.Compression.ZipArchive, which lives
       in the Windows SDK itself. Result: the compile universe contains ONLY Windows SDK
       reference assemblies + Platform.winmd. No packages\ folder is needed anymore;
       packages.config now only pins the .NET Native compiler toolchain package. -->
  <package id="microsoft.net.native.compiler" version="1.7.3" targetFramework="uap10.0.15063" />
</packages>
